+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups, such as `2d`, `1h30m`, or `1d2h3m4s`. The
+units are `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds). Each unit may appear at most once, units must be
+given in that order, and spaces are not allowed. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`. Empty,
+negative, and decimal values are rejected. A {class}`datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is returned
+unchanged. In help output, the metavar is `DURATION`.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1m1h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6.0
+```
+
